<BaseLayout title="Shipping Peptides from China — Customs & Documentation Guide" description="How research peptides are shipped from China: documentation, HS classification, customs risk, restricted destinations and buyer responsibilities." canonical="/knowledge/shipping-peptides-customs-guide/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/knowledge/">Knowledge</a> › Shipping &amp; customs</p>
    <h1>Shipping peptides from China: customs and documentation</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Most shipment failures are paperwork failures. This guide covers what should travel with the goods and what the buyer must confirm before ordering.</p>

    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-shipping-customs-framework/">shipping &amp; customs pillar</a> — the end-to-end export framework.</p>
    <h2>Documents that should accompany the shipment</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Issued by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Commercial invoice</td><td>Value, parties, terms</td><td>Supplier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Packing list</td><td>Units, weights, dimensions</td><td>Supplier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certificate of analysis</td><td>Batch identity and test results</td><td>Supplier QC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MSDS</td><td>Handling and transport classification</td><td>Supplier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certificate of origin</td><td>Origin declaration (if required)</td><td>Chamber / authority</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Air waybill / B/L</td><td>Transport contract</td><td>Carrier</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Classification</h2>
    <p>The HS code depends on the specific compound and its presentation. Buyers should confirm the classification with their customs broker before the first shipment; a wrong code can cause delays, fines or seizure.</p>

    <h2>Restricted destinations</h2>
    <p>Some jurisdictions restrict or prohibit the import of certain peptides. The buyer is responsible for confirming legality. We decline shipments where lawful import cannot be demonstrated.</p>

    <h2>Cold chain</h2>
    <p>Lyophilized peptides are generally stable at ambient temperature for short transit, but summer routes and customs holds can expose them to heat. For sensitive compounds, use insulated packaging with phase-change material and track the shipment.</p>

    <h2>Common delays</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Missing or inconsistent invoice description</li>
      <li>No import permit where one is required</li>
      <li>Customs hold pending additional documentation</li>
      <li>Buyer unavailable for clearance contact</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When a shipment is already held, the response sequence — notice, documents, cold chain and the release/return/abandon decision — is set out in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-customs-hold/">when a shipment is held at customs</a>.</p>

    <div class="ruo">Research use only. This is commercial guidance, not legal advice.</div>


    <h2>Choosing the route</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Transit</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Express courier</td><td>3–7 days</td><td>Small and mid-size orders</td><td>Carrier acceptance rules; limited temperature control</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Air freight</td><td>5–10 days</td><td>Bulk orders, consolidated shipments</td><td>Airport handling; clearance at destination</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sea freight</td><td>Weeks</td><td>Large volume, non-urgent</td><td>Heat and humidity exposure; longer customs window</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The cheapest route is not the lowest-risk route. For a first shipment or a high-value lot, pay for speed and tracking; for routine bulk, sea freight can be rational if the packaging and documentation are strong.</p>

    <h2>The arrival checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Match the batch number on the vial, COA and invoice.</li>
      <li>Inspect seals, crimps and cake appearance.</li>
      <li>Check phase-change packs and any temperature indicator.</li>
      <li>Photograph the packaging before unpacking if there is any damage.</li>
      <li>Record the receipt date and storage location.</li>
      <li>Raise any claim within the contractual window, with evidence.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Arrival is the last chance to document a transit problem. After the packaging is discarded, the evidence is gone.</p>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>The border is where documentation becomes product quality. A shipment that cannot clear is not a logistics delay; it is a failed purchase.</p>

